1 NOTE >>> RESOLVED 29 August 2026: SHE IS RUCHLA, DAUGHTER OF LEWEK. The name is a
2 CONT PATRONYMIC, not a surname, so she correctly has no surname of her own.
2 CONT The key document is Klimontow DEATH ACT 14/1850 (@S1056@), read from the image, which
2 CONT names her plainly as "RUCHLI z LEWKOW" - the short form. Re-read at high magnification,
2 CONT the clearest of the long-form readings, act 27/1854, is LEWKOWICZOW and not
2 CONT "Lemkowiczow": the third letter is a single-humped w. LEWKOW and LEWKOWICZOW are the
2 CONT same patronymic, short and long. That resolves the whole series below in one reading,
2 CONT and it also identifies the "Ryfka z Lewkow" of act 19/1852 as herself - see @I18@.
2 CONT The rival KIMET/KIMEL reading is dropped: the 1852 register uses "z Kimlow" for a
2 CONT DIFFERENT woman on the page facing her own act, which is why the two names kept
2 CONT crossing in my readings.
2 CONT >>> Her father LEWEK is not entered as a person: he is known from this formula alone,
2 CONT with no act, no dates and no other mention. Not to be confused with LEWEK @I1186@ of
2 CONT Checiny - different town, different generation, common name.
1 NOTE THE ORIGINAL CAUTION, KEPT SO THE CORRECTION STAYS LEGIBLE - "MAIDEN NAME UNRESOLVED,
2 CONT and I am recording this as a limit of my own reading rather
2 CONT than pretending to a conclusion. Across seven birth acts read in the original, the
2 CONT maiden-name formula reads: 46/1845 "z Leml(ow)"; 12/1847 illegible; 27/1854
2 CONT "z Lemkowiczow"; 20/1856 "z Kimetow"; 25/1858 no maiden name given; 12/1861
2 CONT "z Komkowiczow"; 19/1863 "z Lemkowiczow" (the clearest of them); 52/1865 "z Lemelow";
2 CONT 42/1867 "z Lemkowiczow". Two live candidates: LEMKOWICZ / LEMEL (five readings, and
2 CONT the clearest single one), or KIMET - and the 1856 act is witnessed by Ejzyk Kimet, the
2 CONT synagogue beadle, which is what a bride's kinsman would be doing there. A Kimet/Kimel
2 CONT family is demonstrably present in Klimontow and witnesses these acts repeatedly.
2 CONT I twice adopted a reading here and twice had to reverse it. It stays open.
1 SOUR @S4@
1 SOUR @S5@
1 SOUR @S6@
1 SOUR @S8@

