1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 JUL 1874
2 PLAC Wojslawice, Chelm, Lublin, Poland
2 NOTE Death act 10/1874 (sygn. 2.1/38, unit 2196589, scan 44, p.77). Reported 15 July at 11am
3 CONT by Sruel Glezer, glazier, 48, and Fajwel Kiczerman, Jewish teacher, 55: "LIBA SZAUFER,
3 CONT widow, SIXTY-FOUR, born in Wojslawice of the late parents GERSZEK and MASZA, lawful
3 CONT spouses GLEZER, living with her relatives."
2 NOTE #proof Probable, close to Proven - see the note below.
1 NOTE _CONF PROVEN as Szmul's mother. FOUR acts name her as Chaim's wife (1838, 1859, 1866,
2 CONT 1872, 1873).
1 NOTE >>> CONFLICT LARGELY RESOLVED 6 Aug 2026, IN FAVOUR OF GLEZER - AND ONE OLD
2 CONT TRANSCRIPTION IN THIS FILE WAS SIMPLY WRONG.
2 CONT Both disputed words were re-read at FULL 3500px resolution (display the image at
2 CONT natural size in the browser and scroll to the word; the screenshot-zoom tool caps out
2 CONT far below this).
2 CONT * Act 14/1859 reads "z KIELNEROW" - unambiguous. It also reads "zyjacych": both Chaim
2 CONT   and Liba were ALIVE in Aug 1859.
2 CONT * Act 9/1866 reads "pozostawiwszy po sobie owdowiala zone LIBE SZAUFEROWA z GLEZEROW".
2 CONT   NOT "nee Kiel[n]er" as previously transcribed - and note the old transcription itself
2 CONT   bracketed the n as uncertain. At 800px it is a scrawl; at 3500px it is Glezerow.
2 CONT SCORE IS NOW 3 GLEZER (1866, 1872, 1874) TO 2 KIELNER (1859, 1873), and the Glezer side
2 CONT holds the two strongest documents: her husband's death act and her own, the latter
2 CONT naming her parents.
2 CONT CORROBORATION, ALL ONE WAY: ages agree exactly (28 in 1838, 64 in 1874, both ~1810);
2 CONT HERSZEK GLEZER, GLAZIER, 50, witnessed her son Lejb's birth in 1838 - the man you bring
2 CONT if he is your father (b. ~1788, she ~1810); her son GERSZ is plausibly named for him;
2 CONT Gersz Glezer, glazier, 46, witnessed that son's marriage in 1872; Gersz lived with his
2 CONT widowed mother in Dec 1872 and the 1874 decedent was "living with her relatives", her
2 CONT death reported by Sruel Glezer, glazier.
2 CONT STILL OPEN: where "Kielner" comes from. Both Kielner acts were drawn by Dawid London,
2 CONT deputy rabbi - but so was act 6/1872, which says Glezer, so "one clerk's habit" does not
2 CONT explain it. Standing hypothesis, NOT adopted: Liba nee Glezer may have been the widow of
2 CONT a KIELNER before marrying Chaim, and some acts recorded that earlier married surname.
2 CONT >>> 6 Aug 2026 - THE KIELNER FAMILY IS NOW IDENTIFIED, AND "KIELNER" IS A REAL SURNAME.
2 CONT Marriage index 1841 (unit 2196505, scan 27) lists, TWO LINES APART IN THE SAME HAND,
2 CONT "KLAJNER Mortko" (act 6) and "KIELNER Zus" (act 10). The clerk distinguishes them, so
2 CONT Kielner is not a mis-spelling of Klajner.
2 CONT Marriage act 10/1841 was then read in full (scan 26 left, p.42): ZUS KIELNER, bachelor,
2 CONT TWENTY, living with his parents in Wojslawice, son of BERKO and BRANDLA, spouses
2 CONT KIELNER of Wojslawice, LIVING FROM MASONRY ("z mularstwa"); his father Berko, mason,
2 CONT present and consenting. Bride Laja Sling, 20, daughter of Ankiel and Ruch[la].
2 CONT ⇒ BERKO AND BRANDLA ARE NOT HERSZKO AND MASZA. Liba's own death act names Herszko and
2 CONT Masza GLEZER. So Liba is NOT a daughter of this Kielner couple, and the GLEZER side
2 CONT still holds. But the "Liba was a Kielner widow" hypothesis now has a concrete family
2 CONT to attach to - and note that death act 2/1844 "KIELNER Brandla" (unit 2196511, already
2 CONT on disk) is almost certainly Berko's wife.
2 CONT >>> AND THAT HYPOTHESIS IS NOW CLOSED, same day. Death act 2/1844 was read (unit
2 CONT 2196511, scan 21 RIGHT, p.49; the scans were already on disk): "on 23 January 1844 died
2 CONT BRANDLA KIELNER of Wojslawice, living from day-labour, FORTY-THREE years of age,
2 CONT daughter of the late KIELMAN and RYFKA, spouses SZNAJDER." Declared by Szaja Szlajn,
2 CONT day-labourer, 36, and Berko Tenenbaum, 45.
2 CONT ⇒ BRANDLA WAS BORN ~1801 - only NINE YEARS BEFORE LIBA. So Liba cannot be her daughter,
2 CONT and a first husband born ~1805 cannot be her son either. THE WHOLE WOJSLAWICE KIELNER
2 CONT HOUSEHOLD (Berko + Brandla, son Zus b. ~1821) IS A GENERATION TOO YOUNG to connect to
2 CONT Liba in any way. The "Kielner widow" idea is dead, and the GLEZER side is stronger
2 CONT still: the only alternative family in town has been ruled out.
2 CONT "Kielner" on Liba is now most likely a clerical error - twice - or an unrecorded
2 CONT earlier Kielner household. Do not re-open without new evidence.
2 CONT ◐ Side detail: act 1 on the same page names a surviving husband; Brandla's act names
2 CONT none, so BERKO KIELNER was probably already dead by Jan 1844 (he was alive Nov 1841).
2 CONT A REJECTED IDEA, recorded so it is not retried: I wondered whether "Kielner" was a
2 CONT misreading of KIESEL, a family in the 1826 Wojslawice death register. The full-resolution
2 CONT re-read of act 14/1859 disproves it - the word is Kielnerow.
2 CONT DECIDING DOCUMENT SOUGHT: the marriage of Chaim and Liba, c.1826-1832.
2 CONT >>> 1826 CHECKED AND NEGATIVE, 6 Aug 2026. The whole 1826 volume (unit 2196493) was read
3 CONT via its three name registers: 18 births (scan 8), TWO marriages (scan 11), 5 deaths
3 CONT (scan 14). The two 1826 marriages are Apfelbaum-Bergerowna and Obell-Bayla Szyfer.
3 CONT Chaim and Liba did NOT marry in Wojslawice in 1826. The 1827-1832 volumes DO NOT EXIST
3 CONT in the fond, so this marriage act is either lost or was drawn up elsewhere.
2 CONT >>> NEW LEAD ON THE MAIDEN NAME: the 1826 death register lists a family "KIESEL"
3 CONT (Kiesel Szloma, act 3). Chaim's 1866 death act was transcribed as "nee Kiel[n]er" with
3 CONT the n marked UNCERTAIN in the hand. If the correct reading is KIESEL, not Kielner, the
3 CONT whole three-act "Kielner" side of this conflict may be one misread name. RE-READ act
3 CONT 9/1866 (unit 2196567, scan 31) and act 14/1859 (unit 2196546, scan 24) at _max. UNDONE.
