1 NOTE >>> HE HAS A GIVEN NAME AND NO SURNAME, AND THAT IS HISTORICALLY CORRECT, NOT A GAP.
2 CONT He belongs to the last generation of Polish Jews before hereditary surnames: the
2 CONT Kingdom of Poland's surname decree is of 27 March 1821, ten years AFTER the act that
2 CONT names him. Men of his lifetime were identified by given name plus patronymic alone.
1 NOTE _CONF #proof Probable as Ita's father. Marriage act 23/1811 calls her "ITY z DUWIDOW"
2 CONT - Ita of the Duwids, i.e. Ita daughter of Duwid. That phrase is the ONLY trace of him.
2 CONT "z Duwidow" is a PATRONYMIC construction, not a family name; DUWID must not be
2 CONT recorded as Ita's maiden surname.
1 NOTE >>> HIS WIFE IS NOT NAMED. Act 23/1811 records only two couples - the bride's parents
2 CONT (Szmul Glezer and Ita) and the groom's (Michlowicz and Estera of Biskupice). The
2 CONT likeliest route to her name is ITA'S OWN DEATH ACT, if she died after 1 Jan 1826 and
2 CONT is therefore in the Jewish register 35/1787, where death acts do name the deceased's
2 CONT parents (compare death act 44/1855, Rechla-Mema, "daughter of CHAIM and LAJA").
2 CONT NOT YET SEARCHED.
1 NOTE >>> POSSIBLE SON, NOT PROVEN: JCEK DAWIDOWICZ, 50 (b. c.1761), of Siedliszcze,
2 CONT witnessed the same wedding. "Dawidowicz" = son of Dawid. He may be this man's son
2 CONT and Ita's brother - but nothing in the act says so, and he is NOT entered.
