2 DATE ABT 1830
2 PLAC Wojslawice, Chelm, Lublin, Poland
2 NOTE "zrodzona z Wojslawic" and living with her parents up to the marriage (act 2/1848).
3 CONT Year estimated from a marriage at the usual age; her stated age in the act is not yet read.
1 NOTE _CONF PROVEN as the daughter of Chaim @I1049@ and Liba @I1050@ - MARRIAGE ACT 2/1848,
2 CONT read in the original 6 Aug 2026 (unit 2196517, scan 16, RIGHT page, p.33; located via the
2 CONT marriage index on scan 18, entry 3: "Kornfeld Wolf z Chaia Fajga Szaufer, akt 2, p.33").
2 CONT She was already listed in the family note of @F1023@ from the JRI index; this is the first
2 CONT reading of the ACT ITSELF, and it upgrades her from an index line to a documented person.
2 CONT "Drawn up in the town of Wojslawice on the twelfth day of [month] 1848 at four o'clock.
2 CONT Appeared the Jew DAWID LONDON, [deputy rabbi] of the Wojslawice parish, together with the
2 CONT Jew WOLF KORNFELD, BACHELOR, DAY-LABOURER in the town of Wojslawice, EIGHTEEN YEARS
2 CONT COMPLETED, born of [.....] and FAJGA, spouses KORNFELD, TRADERS - and with
2 CONT CHAJA FAJGA SZAUFER, SPINSTER, ... years of age, BORN OF CHAIM AND LIBA, NEE UNKNOWN,
2 CONT SPOUSES SZAUFER, DAY-LABOURERS OF WOJSLAWICE, until now residing with her parents."
2 CONT Witnesses/signatories: Dawid London, Wolf Kornfeld, CHAIM SZAUFER (named in the act as
2 CONT "Chaim Szaufer, father of Chaja Fajga Szaufer"), Jojzafa Kierszbaum, Szaja Sztern,
2 CONT day-labourer, twenty-four.
1 NOTE >>> TWO THINGS THIS ACT ADDS BEYOND THE PERSON.
2 CONT (1) OCCUPATION. In 1848 Chaim and Liba are "WYROBNICY" - DAY-LABOURERS. In 1838 Chaim was
2 CONT a "kotlarz" (coppersmith) and by 1866 a house owner. The middle of his life was the poor
2 CONT part. This is the only statement of LIBA's own occupation before her 1872 "rabotnica".
2 CONT (2) ★ THE MAIDEN-NAME CONFLICT. The clerk wrote Liba "Z DOMU NIEWIADOMEJ" - NEE UNKNOWN.
2 CONT In 1848 the Wojslawice registrar did not know, or could not obtain, her maiden name at
2 CONT all. That is a third state alongside GLEZER (1866, 1872, 1874) and KIELNER (1859, 1873),
2 CONT and it weakens any argument that one of the two was the settled, well-known form.
2 CONT See the conflict note at @I1050@.
